[Event "Casual game"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "1851.06.21"]
[White "Anderssen, Adolf"]
[Black "Kieseritzky, Lionel"]
[Result "1-0"]

{The Immortal Game. White gives up a bishop, both rooks and the queen, and
mates with the three minor pieces that are left. It was a casual game, played
during a break in a tournament, and it is the most famous game ever played.}
1. e4 e5 2. f4 {The King's Gambit: a pawn offered to rip open the centre.
Respectable in 1851, and still played by people who enjoy themselves.
[%cal Gf2f4]} exf4 3. Bc4 Qh4+ 4. Kf1 {White cannot castle for the rest of the
game, and does not appear to mind.} b5 5. Bxb5 Nf6 6. Nf3 Qh6 7. d3 Nh5 8. Nh4
Qg5 9. Nf5 c6 10. g4 {Attacking the knight and shutting the queen in at the
same time. [%cal Gg2g4][%csl Rh5]} Nf6 11. Rg1 {Offering the bishop \u2014 the first
of the sacrifices.} cxb5 12. h4 Qg6 13. h5 Qg5 14. Qf3 Ng8 {Black has taken a
bishop and driven the knight home, but every black piece is now on its
starting square except the queen.} 15. Bxf4 Qf6 16. Nc3 Bc5 17. Nd5 {Offering
both rooks.} Qxb2 18. Bd6 {And the second rook. Black cannot take the bishop
with the queen: 18... Qxa1+ 19. Ke2 and the mate is coming anyway.
[%cal Gf4d6]} Bxg1 19. e5 Qxa1+ 20. Ke2 Na6 21. Nxg7+ Kd8 22. Qf6+ {The queen
too. [%cal Gf3f6][%csl Rf6]} Nxf6 23. Be7# {Mate, with a bishop and two
knights, against a queen, two rooks and a bishop. [%csl Ge7,Gd5,Gg7]} 1-0
